1
00:00:11,010 --> 00:00:13,720
Sheldon, it's not a great time!

2
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:15,500
What do you want?

3
00:00:15,570 --> 00:00:18,430
Hello to you, too.

4
00:00:18,430 --> 00:00:21,410
I'm sorry, but this is important.

5
00:00:21,470 --> 00:00:23,110
What is it?

6
00:00:23,110 --> 00:00:24,670
Back to the Future II was

7
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:27,580
in the Back to the Future III case...

8
00:00:27,580 --> 00:00:30,050
and Back to the Future III was-- get this--

9
00:00:30,050 --> 00:00:33,140
in the Back to the Future II case.

10
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:34,680
So?

11
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:37,380
So, did you do that,

12
00:00:37,380 --> 00:00:39,860
or am I in the house with an intruder?

13
00:00:40,980 --> 00:00:43,070
Sheldon, I got to go inside.

14
00:00:43,070 --> 00:00:45,760
It's getting rough out here.

15
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:48,010
You're dodging the question;

16
00:00:48,010 --> 00:00:49,730
I knew it was you.

17
00:00:53,270 --> 00:00:54,890
What was that?

18
00:00:54,890 --> 00:00:56,080
What was what?

19
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:57,520
This isn't a very good connection,

20
00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:00,970
but it sounded like someone just released a kraken.

21
00:01:02,210 --> 00:01:05,710
Okay, I'm hanging up now.

22
00:01:05,710 --> 00:01:07,080
You know there's no such thing as a...

23
00:01:10,110 --> 00:01:12,920
No...!

24
00:01:13,610 --> 00:01:15,720
Penny... Penny...

25
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:17,460
Penny...!

26
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:19,880
What's the matter?

27
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:22,920
Um... well, I was worried

28
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:25,920
that you might be missing Leonard.

29
00:01:26,460 --> 00:01:29,180
And that might be causing you to have bad dreams,

30
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:31,530
like the kind you'd get if you watched

31
00:01:31,530 --> 00:01:34,830
Clash of the Titans right before you went to bed.

32
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:38,940
Sweetie, did you have a bad dream?

33
00:01:39,230 --> 00:01:40,810
To be honest, I did.

34
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:42,890
Back to the Future II was

35
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:46,110
in the Back to the Future III case.

36
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:48,230
Leonard did it.

37
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:49,200
Good night.

38
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:50,690
No, wait.

39
00:01:51,330 --> 00:01:53,500
Perhaps I should sleep here

40
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:56,380
so you don't miss Leonard as much,

41
00:01:56,380 --> 00:01:59,130
uh, 'cause you're being kind of a baby about it.

42
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,500
You know what? That would make me feel better.

43
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:08,390
- Thank you. - You're welcome.

44
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:14,800
Good night.

45
00:02:42,230 --> 00:02:43,780
I think you'd be pleased to hear

46
00:02:43,780 --> 00:02:45,500
that this morning in the parking garage,

47
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:47,100
I saw this oil stain on the ground

48
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:49,780
that was shaped just like my ex-girlfriend, Lucy,

49
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:52,570
and I didn't get upset at all.

50
00:02:53,650 --> 00:02:54,520
I'm proud of you.

51
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:57,880
Well, you should be, 'cause she was looking good.

52
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:01,150
Dear Lord! You're an astronomer.

53
00:03:01,150 --> 00:03:03,030
Although you may have earthly woes,

54
00:03:03,030 --> 00:03:05,350
get your mind back on the stars.

55
00:03:05,350 --> 00:03:07,110
Gee, even the lowly dung beetle

56
00:03:07,110 --> 00:03:10,220
chooses to plot its course by using the Milky Way.

57
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:11,660
Is that true?

58
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:14,010
Everything I say is true.

59
00:03:14,010 --> 00:03:16,120
Now, of course, the dung beetle also enjoys

60
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:18,530
eating feces, living in feces

61
00:03:18,530 --> 00:03:20,790
and making little balls out of feces.

62
00:03:20,790 --> 00:03:22,900
So pick and choose

63
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:26,570
which aspects of its lifestyle you want to embrace.

64
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:28,800
Come on, you can talk to girls now.

65
00:03:28,810 --> 00:03:30,960
It shouldn't be hard for you to meet someone new.

66
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:32,140
How can I meet someone new

67
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:35,040
when everywhere I look, I see Lucy's face?

68
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:36,660
Tell me you don't see her smile

69
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:39,270
in the crust of this chicken pot pie.

70
00:03:40,370 --> 00:03:42,120
Oh, will you stop it!

71
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:47,390
Now, listen to me.

72
00:03:47,390 --> 00:03:49,730
There's a welcome party for incoming post-docs tonight.

73
00:03:49,730 --> 00:03:51,230
Go to it and meet someone

74
00:03:51,230 --> 00:03:54,580
who isn't made of grease or pie.

75
00:03:54,580 --> 00:03:57,870
You think you're so cool because your wife is a person?

76
00:04:01,390 --> 00:04:02,240
Look,

77
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:04,300
Bernie's at a neuroscience conference with Amy--

78
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:05,360
I'll go with you.

79
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:06,740
- You would do that for me? - Of course.

80
00:04:06,740 --> 00:04:07,490
You're my friend.

81
00:04:07,490 --> 00:04:09,180
I want you to be happy.

82
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:10,620
- Thanks. - Oh, Sheldon,

83
00:04:10,620 --> 00:04:13,170
since Amy's out of town, would you like to join us?

84
00:04:13,580 --> 00:04:15,750
I want you to be happy, too,

85
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:18,680
but not enough to do anything about it.

86
00:04:21,690 --> 00:04:22,750
Howie, stop.

87
00:04:22,750 --> 00:04:23,980
I can't talk like that.

88
00:04:23,980 --> 00:04:26,240
Amy's right here.

89
00:04:26,870 --> 00:04:28,760
Sheldon, stop. For the last time,

90
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:32,040
I will not bring home bed bugs.

91
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:33,040
The hotel's nice.

92
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:35,110
There's a pool, a gym--

93
00:04:35,110 --> 00:04:36,510
the bar looks like fun.

94
00:04:36,510 --> 00:04:40,350
Because I looked in the bed, and there are no bugs.

95
00:04:40,350 --> 00:04:42,200
Aw, I love you, too.

96
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:43,890
If I don't talk to you before you go to sleep,

97
00:04:43,890 --> 00:04:46,320
I'll meet you in dreamland.

98
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:47,900
Good night.

99
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:51,620
No, I will not consider sleeping in my garment bag.

100
00:04:54,550 --> 00:04:57,970
Penny, did you ever wonder how Starfleet captains...

101
00:04:57,970 --> 00:05:00,100
No.

102
00:05:02,040 --> 00:05:04,160
Well, now that I've piqued your interest,

103
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:07,690
welcome to the exciting world of 3D chess.

104
00:05:07,690 --> 00:05:08,630
Why don't you just admit

105
00:05:08,630 --> 00:05:10,070
you only want to play this game

106
00:05:10,070 --> 00:05:11,650
because you always play it with Leonard

107
00:05:11,650 --> 00:05:13,490
and you miss him?

108
00:05:13,490 --> 00:05:16,680
You overestimate his significance in my life.

109
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:19,770
Do I miss how he makes a face

110
00:05:19,770 --> 00:05:22,140
on my toaster waffle with syrup?

111
00:05:22,730 --> 00:05:23,880
No.

112
00:05:24,900 --> 00:05:25,950
Do I miss the way

113
00:05:25,950 --> 00:05:29,090
he fixes the zipper on my jacket when it gets stuck?

114
00:05:29,090 --> 00:05:31,560
I don't think so.

115
00:05:31,560 --> 00:05:33,120
Do I miss how we say "good night"

116
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:35,450
to each other through the walls of our bedroom

117
00:05:35,450 --> 00:05:37,090
using Morse code?

118
00:05:41,620 --> 00:05:43,040
Okay, I get it, I get it.

119
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:44,910
You are an emotionless robot.

120
00:05:44,910 --> 00:05:47,120
Well, I try.

121
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:49,960
All right, let's just get this stupid game over with.

122
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:51,890
- Great. I'll go first. - Okay.

123
00:05:51,890 --> 00:05:53,430
By the way, how are you with zippers?

124
00:05:53,430 --> 00:05:54,030
Why?

125
00:05:54,030 --> 00:05:55,620
Well, I really need to go to the bathroom,

126
00:05:55,620 --> 00:05:58,320
and this one's gone all cattywampus.

127
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:04,030
Wow, your work on jellyfish neural nets

128
00:06:04,030 --> 00:06:05,700
sounds so interesting.

129
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:06,720
It is.

130
00:06:06,720 --> 00:06:09,400
You can download my paper off the university server.

131
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:11,570
I will. You can download my paper

132
00:06:11,570 --> 00:06:14,440
on the Van Allen Belts from the university server as well.

133
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:16,190
I will.

134
00:06:18,470 --> 00:06:20,090
All right.

135
00:06:34,260 --> 00:06:35,130
How'd it go?

136
00:06:35,130 --> 00:06:37,240
Well, if you like dry, factual statements

137
00:06:37,240 --> 00:06:39,240
interspersed with painful moments of silence,

138
00:06:39,240 --> 00:06:41,550
it was bananas.

139
00:06:42,810 --> 00:06:43,450
Check it out.

140
00:06:43,450 --> 00:06:46,060
Mrs. Davis from Human Resources is here.

141
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:48,380
She's probably on the lookout for sexual harassment.

142
00:06:48,380 --> 00:06:49,380
Oh, great.

143
00:06:49,380 --> 00:06:52,400
There go my chances of being sexually harassed.

144
00:06:53,360 --> 00:06:57,160
I heard her husband left her for a hot, young undergrad.

145
00:06:57,160 --> 00:06:59,780
That's so much better than the old, ugly ones.

146
00:07:05,110 --> 00:07:06,110
Mr. Wolowitz,

147
00:07:06,110 --> 00:07:07,260
Dr. Koothrappali.

148
00:07:07,260 --> 00:07:09,190
Mrs. Davis, nice to see you.

149
00:07:09,890 --> 00:07:12,380
You know, I recently read a fascinating article

150
00:07:12,380 --> 00:07:15,050
on infidelity among penguins.

151
00:07:16,240 --> 00:07:17,670
Okay.

152
00:07:17,670 --> 00:07:20,250
So if the fact that your husband left you

153
00:07:20,250 --> 00:07:22,060
makes you feel unattractive, just remember,

154
00:07:22,060 --> 00:07:25,880
penguins get cheated on, and they're adorable.

155
00:07:38,020 --> 00:07:41,010
It was better when you couldn't talk to women.

156
00:07:43,700 --> 00:07:45,000
I was thinking of going to the lecture

157
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:46,300
on posterior cingulate cortex lesions

158
00:07:46,300 --> 00:07:49,200
in the formation of autobiographical memory.

159
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:51,190
Oh, brain lesions are fascinating.

160
00:07:51,190 --> 00:07:53,990
Unless they're yours, then they're a drag.

161
00:07:55,470 --> 00:07:57,100
To the advancement of science.

162
00:07:57,100 --> 00:08:00,100
And to the sick and dying who make it possible.

163
00:08:02,160 --> 00:08:03,950
This is fun; we never really get to talk shop

164
00:08:03,950 --> 00:08:05,040
with Penny around.

165
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:07,140
We usually just end up talking about boys.

166
00:08:07,140 --> 00:08:09,140
Which is fine, but it's nice to mix it up

167
00:08:09,140 --> 00:08:11,650
with a little intellectual conversation.

168
00:08:11,650 --> 00:08:13,820
From the two gentlemen at the bar.

169
00:08:13,820 --> 00:08:15,420
Oh, my gosh, boys bought us drinks!

170
00:08:15,420 --> 00:08:17,040
Boys bought us drinks!

171
00:08:17,960 --> 00:08:20,510
Thank you, thank you, thank you so much.

172
00:08:21,780 --> 00:08:23,340
- Be cool. - You be cool!

173
00:08:23,340 --> 00:08:26,290
Guys are hitting on us, and not just to get to Penny.

174
00:08:27,050 --> 00:08:28,820
You're right.

175
00:08:28,820 --> 00:08:30,270
Thank you.

176
00:08:35,250 --> 00:08:37,450
Bad move.

177
00:08:38,490 --> 00:08:39,610
Really? Why?

178
00:08:39,610 --> 00:08:43,040
My queen can now take your rook from below.

179
00:08:43,040 --> 00:08:44,560
So that means I lose, right?

180
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:46,400
It's over!

181
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:49,830
If I make this move...

182
00:08:49,830 --> 00:08:52,690
but I won't because we're having too much fun.

183
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:56,930
Okay, let's take a break.

184
00:08:56,930 --> 00:08:59,690
We're all out of alcohol.

185
00:08:59,690 --> 00:09:01,360
I wasn't going to get alcohol.

186
00:09:08,740 --> 00:09:10,180
Gosh, I wonder what Leonard's doing right now.

187
00:09:10,180 --> 00:09:11,980
I miss him so much.

188
00:09:11,980 --> 00:09:13,710
Well, if you'd like,

189
00:09:13,710 --> 00:09:15,180
we could call him.

190
00:09:15,180 --> 00:09:17,220
I-I mean that you could call him.

191
00:09:17,220 --> 00:09:18,390
As I've explained,

192
00:09:18,390 --> 00:09:20,840
the absence of my friends does not cause me pain.

193
00:09:20,840 --> 00:09:22,890
As rock and roll bad boy, Paul Simon, once said,

194
00:09:22,890 --> 00:09:23,920
"I am a rock.

195
00:09:23,920 --> 00:09:26,170
I am an I-I-I-island."

196
00:09:28,410 --> 00:09:29,110
I'm calling him.

197
00:09:29,110 --> 00:09:31,020
Oh, goodie! Put him on speakerphone!

198
00:09:39,430 --> 00:09:40,660
Excuse me, ladies,

199
00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:43,010
but my pants are buzzing.

200
00:09:44,950 --> 00:09:47,550
North Sea, how can I "Kelp" You?

201
00:09:49,240 --> 00:09:50,410
Leonard?

202
00:09:50,410 --> 00:09:51,450
Penny?

203
00:09:51,450 --> 00:09:52,310
Hey, it's Penny.

204
00:09:52,310 --> 00:09:53,910
Everybody say hi to Penny.

205
00:09:53,910 --> 00:09:56,340
Hi, Penny!

206
00:09:56,580 --> 00:09:59,260
Wow, it sounds like you're having a good time.

207
00:09:59,260 --> 00:10:02,420
Best time of my life!

208
00:10:04,480 --> 00:10:06,840
Isn't it 5:30 in the morning there?

209
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:07,850
Is it?

210
00:10:07,850 --> 00:10:10,930
Hey, everybody, it's 5:30 in the morning!

211
00:10:14,210 --> 00:10:15,960
okay, well, we were just calling you

212
00:10:15,960 --> 00:10:17,330
because we were missing you.

213
00:10:17,330 --> 00:10:20,230
- Iceberg! - Uh-oh, hang on.

214
00:10:20,230 --> 00:10:21,550
Are you in danger?

215
00:10:21,550 --> 00:10:23,030
No, it's a drinking game.

216
00:10:23,030 --> 00:10:25,910
Whenever we see an iceberg, we take a shot!

217
00:10:25,910 --> 00:10:27,180
Berg! Berg!

218
00:10:27,180 --> 00:10:30,130
Berg! Berg! Berg! Berg--

219
00:10:30,130 --> 00:10:32,530
Leonard, Leonard?

220
00:10:34,740 --> 00:10:37,770
I cannot believe we were missing that jerk.

221
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:40,210
You were.

222
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:46,560
So what happened? How'd it go?

223
00:10:46,560 --> 00:10:47,850
It's fine. I thanked them again

224
00:10:47,850 --> 00:10:49,850
and let them know we're not available.

225
00:10:49,850 --> 00:10:51,500
So I can drink this without having

226
00:10:51,500 --> 00:10:53,600
to give up the goodies?

227
00:10:53,600 --> 00:10:55,140
Yes, it's all cool.

228
00:10:56,250 --> 00:10:57,870
Although, if you wanted to talk to one of them,

229
00:10:57,870 --> 00:10:59,910
no one would blame you.

230
00:10:59,910 --> 00:11:02,600
And why would no one blame me?

231
00:11:03,480 --> 00:11:05,840
I don't know what I'm saying.

232
00:11:06,950 --> 00:11:08,820
Well, it sounds like you're saying

233
00:11:08,820 --> 00:11:10,670
that I could do better than Sheldon.

234
00:11:10,670 --> 00:11:12,960
Boy, these drinks are strong!

235
00:11:14,650 --> 00:11:18,770
Hoo, mama, I'm gonna be hug in the toilet tonight!

236
00:11:20,330 --> 00:11:20,940
No, tell me,

237
00:11:20,940 --> 00:11:23,800
I want to know what you meant by that.

238
00:11:25,080 --> 00:11:27,430
I just meant that you're not married

239
00:11:27,430 --> 00:11:29,520
and your boyfriend's kind of...

240
00:11:29,520 --> 00:11:31,440
Sheldon.

241
00:11:32,590 --> 00:11:34,760
And your husband is extremely Howard.

242
00:11:34,760 --> 00:11:36,760
What's your point?

243
00:11:38,090 --> 00:11:39,970
Sorry. I have no point.

244
00:11:39,970 --> 00:11:41,830
That was a stupid thing to say.

245
00:11:41,830 --> 00:11:44,090
Can we please just go back to having a nice time?

246
00:11:44,090 --> 00:11:47,240
We could, but unfortunately my brain is lesion-free

247
00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:48,560
and I remember that rotten thing

248
00:11:48,560 --> 00:11:50,560
you just said about my sweet baboo.

249
00:11:51,710 --> 00:11:52,650
Come on. I apologize.

250
00:11:52,650 --> 00:11:54,830
Can we please just let it go?

251
00:11:55,690 --> 00:11:58,080
- Sure. - Thanks.

252
00:11:59,760 --> 00:12:02,890
Your husband's weird and his clothes are ridiculous.

253
00:12:04,560 --> 00:12:05,840
I can't believe it!

254
00:12:05,840 --> 00:12:07,170
All this time I've been doing nothing

255
00:12:07,170 --> 00:12:08,940
but sit around and miss that guy.

256
00:12:08,940 --> 00:12:10,360
And you know what the worst part is?

257
00:12:10,360 --> 00:12:11,860
That you're having to process

258
00:12:11,860 --> 00:12:14,700
your emotional pain without vodka?

259
00:12:19,490 --> 00:12:21,560
No.

260
00:12:21,560 --> 00:12:23,570
Yes.

261
00:12:24,570 --> 00:12:26,560
But you know what the second-worst part is?

262
00:12:26,560 --> 00:12:28,150
He does not miss me at all.

263
00:12:28,150 --> 00:12:31,130
Allow me to comfort you.

264
00:12:31,130 --> 00:12:33,670
At least you've got your health.

265
00:12:36,880 --> 00:12:39,720
Really? Tha-That's it? That's comforting?

266
00:12:39,720 --> 00:12:43,070
Um, uh, in a hundred years,

267
00:12:43,070 --> 00:12:46,350
you'll both be dead and it won't matter?

268
00:12:47,560 --> 00:12:48,410
No! Come on!

269
00:12:48,410 --> 00:12:50,970
You're supposed to say, "Of course he misses you.

270
00:12:50,970 --> 00:12:54,550
The only reason he's partying is to cover up his pain."

271
00:12:54,550 --> 00:12:56,470
Oh, no, I don't think that's true at all.

272
00:12:59,220 --> 00:13:00,460
This is ridiculous.

